<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Lament · 1884 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Oh My Darling, Clementine</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A comic American folk song of the 1880s about Clementine, a gold miner's daughter, and the sweetheart who mourns her.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Percy Montrose (or Montross); also credited to Barker Bradford</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>American (the songs of the West and the California Gold Rush)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>United States (published in Boston)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>published 1884; based on a song printed in 1863</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words credited to Percy Montrose, published 1884 (and to Barker Bradford, 1885), after a song printed in 1863)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a>&quot; (Roud 9611, sometimes simply &quot;Clementine&quot;) is a traditional American Western folk ballad, tragic but sometimes comic, in trochaic metre. It is usually credited to Percy Montrose (or Montross), 1884, and sometimes to Barker Bradford, 1885. Its many versions all centre on Clementine, the daughter of a &quot;miner forty-niner&quot; and the singer's sweetheart, who falls into a raging current one day while doing her chores and drowns, because her lover cannot swim. Members of the Western Writers of America chose it as one of the top 100 Western songs of all time.</p><p>Montrose's words were issued as sheet music by Oliver Ditson &amp; Co of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1884, based on an earlier song, &quot;Down by the River Liv'd a Maiden&quot;, printed in 1863. The origin of the melody is unknown. In his book South from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, Gerald Brenan claims that it came from an old Spanish ballad made popular by Mexican miners during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> Gold Rush, best known from the &quot;Romance del Conde Olinos&quot;, a sad love story very popular in Spanish-speaking cultures.</p><p>It is not clear when, where and by whom the song was first recorded in English, but the first version to reach the Billboard charts was Bing Crosby's, recorded on 14 June 1941, which briefly reached No. 20: an up-tempo arrangement by Hal Hopper and John Scott Trotter with five swinging verses of new words, one of them mentioning Gene Autry. Jan and Dean's &quot;Clementine&quot;, released on the Dore label in November 1959, reached No. 65 on the Billboard Hot 100. Bobby Darin's version of 1960, with words credited to Woody Harris, turned Clementine into a woman of 299 pounds who might be mistaken for a whale, and reached No. 12 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Tom Lehrer recorded a set of variations on the song in 1959, on An Evening Wasted with Tom Lehrer, to show his theory that &quot;folk songs are so atrocious because they were written by the people&quot;, singing its verses in the styles of Cole Porter, Mozart, bebop and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> and Sullivan.</p><p>The tune travelled far. In 1961, when the ethnomusicologist Colin Turnbull asked a group of Ituri pygmies to sing the oldest song they knew, they sang to the tune of &quot;Clementine&quot;. It carries the New Year song &quot;Xīnnián Hǎo&quot;, sung at Chinese New Year, the Jewish New Year song &quot;Dip the Apple in the Honey&quot;, &quot;Yeh Hai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> Meri Jaan&quot; in the Hindi film C.I.D. (1956), the Indonesian children's song &quot;Makan Apa&quot; and a drinking song of Scandinavian student societies. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, Christodoulos Papachrysostomou set to it &quot;It Was the First of April&quot;, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoka">EOKA</a> song commemorating the start of the EOKA uprising against British colonial rule on 1 April 1955. Its melody was the theme of the North Korean film The Flower Girl (1972), whose music was claimed to be the work of Kim Il-sung, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-orwell">George Orwell</a>'s Animal Farm (1945) the tune of &quot;Beasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>&quot; is described as something between &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-cucaracha">La Cucaracha</a>&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a>&quot;.</p><p>It runs through John Ford's film My Darling Clementine (1946), with Henry Fonda, from its opening credits on, and townspeople sing it before a screening in Hud (1963); a garbled version was the signature tune of the cartoon dog Huckleberry Hound. In 1992 Peter Brooke, Secretary of State for Northern Ireland, sang &quot;Darlin' Clementine&quot; on The Late Late Show on Irish television only hours after the Teebane bombing had killed eight people, seven of them civilians; he was forced to resign shortly afterwards.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A mock-sad ballad. The singer remembers his sweetheart Clementine, the daughter of a gold miner, describes her with affectionate exaggeration, and tells how she slipped into the river while driving her ducklings to the water and drowned, since he could not swim to save her. The chorus mourns her as lost and gone for ever, and the last verses turn the grief into a joke. The mood is a lament played for laughs.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In a cavern, in a canon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Excavating for a mine.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dwelt a miner, forty-niner,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And his daughter, Clementine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Light she was, and like a fairy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And her shoes were number nine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Herring boxes, without topses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sandals were for Clementine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Drove she ducklings to the water,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ev’ry morning just at nine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hit her foot against a splinter</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Fell into the foaming brine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ruby lips above the water,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Blowing bubbles, soft and fine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Alas, for me! I was no swimmer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So I lost my Clementine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In a churchyard, near the canon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the myrtle boughs entwine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There grow roses, and other posies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Fertilized by Clementine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then the miner, forty-niner,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Soon began to peak and pine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thought he “oughter jine” his daughter,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now he’s with his Clementine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In my dreams she still doth haunt me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Robed in garments soaked in brine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Though in life I used to hug her,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now she’s dead, I’ll draw the line.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh my darling, Oh my darling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh my darling Clementine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>You are lost and gone forever,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dreadful sorry, Clementine.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Words and music credited to Percy Montrose (1884), as printed in the songbook College Songs on English Wikisource. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/College_Songs%2FOh_My_Darling_Clementine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="h-vaqAcC5Tg" data-title="Oh My Darling, Clementine: Pete Seeger, voice and banjo"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Pete Seeger, voice and banjo</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Pete Seeger - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h-vaqAcC5Tg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Clementine (Bing Crosby, arranged by Hal Hopper and John Scott Trotter)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, swing arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Clementine (Jan and Dean)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Clementine (Bobby Darin, words credited to Woody Harris)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">variations on Clementine (Tom Lehrer, An Evening Wasted with Tom Lehrer)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody in the styles of other composers</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Clementine (Elliott Smith)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1995</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in: a bartender sings it in his song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Animal Farm (George Orwell)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1945</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its tune named for &quot;Beasts of England&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My Darling Clementine (film, John Ford)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1946</span><span class="sg-chain-h">opening credits and score</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Yeh Hai Bombay Meri Jaan (C.I.D.)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1956</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Flower Girl (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1972</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, as its theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">It Was the First of April (Christodoulos Papachrysostomou)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, an EOKA song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Xīnnián Hǎo</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, a New Year song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dip the Apple in the Honey</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, a Jewish New Year song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Makan Apa</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, an Indonesian children's song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Full i dag og Full i morgen</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, a Scandinavian student drinking song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Swagger Jagger (Cher Lloyd)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its chorus seen as heavily borrowed from the melody</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Down by the River Liv'd a Maiden</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1863</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the earlier song Montrose's words were based on</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Romance del Conde Olinos</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the Spanish ballad Gerald Brenan claims as the melody's source</span></li></ul></section>
